        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
This experiment record is for the ground-based study. For the flight version of the experiment, please refer to LSDA ID Lightening_Effects. 
<p>
This research is comprised of a multidisciplinary collaboration between Thomas Jefferson University, Brigham and Women’s Hospital, and Johnson Space Center (JSC) to complete a ground-based study in a high fidelity analog of the crew sleeping quarters and daily living environment of the International Space Station (ISS). Specifically, standardized psychometric, physiological and neurobehavioral measures will test the efficacy of light from the Solid-State Lighting Assembly (SSLA) to improve vision, circadian regulation, sleep and performance in healthy astronaut-aged subjects.
<p>
APPROACH:<br>
For testing, a controlled five-day inpatient study will be conducted with 28 healthy, male and female, astronaut-aged subjects. Subjects will have their own living area connected to a bathroom/shower facility. Ambient light in these facilities will be produced by ceiling-mounted white fluorescent lamps (4500 K) that are set to a mean luminance of 210 cd at subjects’ eye level when standing (0°). The facilities are equipped with high fidelity replicas of ISS CQs with interior lighting provided by SSLA prototypes. Prior to admission into the study facility, subjects will maintain a consistent sleep-wake schedule monitored by actigraphy, sleep logs and timed phone call-ins for two weeks. After entering the laboratory, subjects will continue to wear an actigraph and live on a typical ISS workday (18 hours wake: 6 hours of sleep) in an isolated windowless environment with direct or intercom contact with the study staff. Groups of 14 volunteers each will be exposed to either 1) the Dynamic Lighting  Schedule protocol based on a morning alerting stimulus upon awakening (6500 K, 420 cd), a general  illumination stimulus (4500 K, 210 cd) during the daytime, and an evening pre-sleep stimulus (2700 K, 7.7 cd), or 2) a static, daily lighting of general illumination only. The team of investigators worked with the project management team at JSC to determine the utility of lowering the nominal SSLA pre-sleep setting from 90 cd to 7.7 cd for the analog study.
<p>
RESULTS:<br>
This experiment is in progress. Results will be available at a later date.
<p>
